so the yield photo drummer project  about three years ago when Lauren Tilton  who is a graduate student in the  American Studies department was working  on a project where she was using  photographs from the FSA owi collection  which is a collection of 165,000  photographs that were taken by the US  government between nineteen thirty-five  and 1945 and because they were taken by  the federal government they're amazingly  open source and because they're housed  at the Library of Congress which is  awesome they digitize them all and so  you can go online and see all 165,000  images and all of the metadata that's  also attached to them so which what's  really great is that since these were  all made at the same time by the same  group there are a lot of pieces of  metadata that like basically when you're  when you're trying to apply something  such as Dublin core to all the paintings  in the Louvre or something there they're  naturally going to be pieces of metadata  that are specific to certain paintings  and not specific to others what's great  about this collection is there are  things such as something called a lot  number which is the assignment that a  photographer was on there's a certain  classification hierarchical  classification system which I'll show  you a visualization of so what's really  great about this collection is that it  has fantastic metadata it's all  digitized and it's all open source  wasn't fantastic about it at least when  Lauren was working on it is that the  only way to explore the collection is to  go on to the Library of Congress website  and just search for things there's like  Google you know Google style search for  something and you can type in cows and  you'll see pictures of cows pictures of  cheese if you want to see them if you  know a photographer or a place you can  take those all in and that's really  great if your research concerns one  specific location or one specific type  of photo or sometimes like Migrant  Mother if you're familiar with that one  is in this collection sometimes people  are interested in one specific photo and  that's great but Lauren studies more  like representation of like race and  ethnicity and she was interested in how  the collection as a whole was actually  characterizing race in the United States  during the Great Depression and World  War two and for her it wasn't enough to  look at the individual  photos she was actually interested in  the whole collection and and what was  the collection as sort of looking at  patterns and things what the collection  was showing her and so as a statistician  she was showing me this and I'm thinking  open data and I can download it and  there's there's a lot of it and I was  just like giddy and so the project sort  of came from that and we downloaded it  and started playing around with it and  making visualizations and as we did that  it was just there were more deep  questions and more exciting things that  we learned about it and so from that  point we actually started developing a  team and I think that's the theme have  been hearing at this whole this whole  day is that you know digital humanities  it's all about the team and so we got  the buy-in from Laura Wexler who's a  professor of American Studies and is  Lauren is adviser to direct the project  we had Stacie maples who's in the map  collection here Kim panko who was that  who was formerly at Yale instructional  technology I'm and then Peter Leonard  when he when he came here has also  joined our team and then scrolling down  we also got we went we actually went to  the physical library of congress and  talked to the people who are curating  them and asked them you know like if you  could visualize this how would you do it  and what are things that we don't know  there are a lot of the metadata wasn't  very well documented but it was there  which is great and we were able to work  with them and do things that they  couldn't do because they have a lot of  restrictions because of like 88  compliance and time resources and so  they were really happy to work with us  and we won a National Endowment for the  Humanities grant and so all of this came  together and now we're actually at the  point this fall where we have a working  website it's at photo grammar dot yelled  edu it's sort of in a still testing  phase but we're kind of a soft open so  before it was behind a firewall and now  you can share it and look at it and  we're trying to get advice from people  about you know how could you add what do  we have to explain a little bit better  load testing and all of these things  before making sort of an official  announcement and putting it out to the  public at large so I thought I would  just spend a little bit of time showing  you a demo sort of the way that that we  visualize people going  through the the website so probably the  most exciting thing is the map so this  is a chloroplast map of all the counties  in the US where photographs from this  collection were taken so you can see  some of them are shaded very heavily and  that's where there are a lot of photos  and some of them are shaded more lightly  that's where there weren't quite as many  sure so one thing you can do is that  like I said the screens cutting me off a  little bit but I can limit the years and  only look at say the first year of the  collection and I can see that it was  much more concentrated in the rural  areas less so than the cities and for  instance when I go and look at the later  years when they were losing funding we  see they were mostly in the neighborhood  of DC which was cheaper and easier to  photograph and so putting the whole nap  back what we've observed when we were  doing using user testing is that  everybody's first thing they do is they  try to find their hometown which makes a  lot of sense and it's one of the  exciting things about this collection is  that it spans most of the u.s. so my  hometown is in Brunswick Maine justing  in Cumberland County so I can click on  that and I see that there are 43 photos  so clicking in I can actually see all of  the photos here zoom out oh there we go  and so you can see that these are all  the photographs that were taken and  digitizing the collection and they range  from things such as like the wharf in  Portland which is now all restaurants  flooded homes in sebago lake and then my  favorite is the Hermit of Maine which is  this pipe smoking organist who lives all  alone somewhere in Maine and so you can  start to people start to connect with  this collection and I think that's why  it's been so popular is because  everybody came from somewhere and if  you're from the US and you're not from  one of these random spots where they  didn't take photographs there are going  to be photos that you know and places  that you've seen you know 70 80 years  are going and it's just really exciting  to see that so as everything there's a  lot of metadata attached to this  collection that that is special to this  collection so one of them that's kind of  the most exciting is on these  classification tags which  hierarchical classification of subject  classification of the photographs in the  collection so for instance this is  categorized people as such groups and  individuals middle-aged men and so if we  wanted to see other middle-aged men we  could click on this and see all of them  are we were one there's also an old man  and really old man and we were wondering  who decided where the cut-offs were  there and how political that got but and  so you can see all the photographs that  are that are stored like this and from a  metadata MLS side thing this is really  interesting because these are the  categories that they're the physical  prints are stored out in the library of  congress so now in nowadays in computing  we can do like Peter does you know add a  bunch of keywords in search and we can  we can have a photograph exist in seven  different categories or a hundred  different categories but at the time  this one photograph had to go in one  drawer and so this was the  categorization they actually used for it  and so if instead of so one way to drill  into the collection is to start at the  map and then go to a specific  photographing and see similar ones but  another way of doing it is to look at  the whole set of classification tags and  drill down so for instance these are the  the top-level category so we could click  on transportation and then let's say we  wanted tourists because I'm not really  sure how that's transportation and then  so I guess there's only one sub category  in there and then we'll see the 18  photos that are in that folder and if  you go to the library of congress it's  actually pretty exciting you can go and  open the right drawer and the right  binder and pull them out and you'll see  presumably as long as they haven't  gotten miss or miss organize these 18  photos that are in there um so it's  pretty interesting when you go there and  put the collection back together with  the website but this lets you do it from  anywhere instead of having to go  physically to DC to do it and so the  last thing I'll show you which is which  is actually added a lot more context to  this than I think we even realized that  it would is so these are chloroplasts  and what we this record with Matt where  we've shaded each county by how many  photos there are but another way to do  it is simply to put it dot where every  photograph where every photograph was  taken and it's a pretty big jumble but  I can get it in here so but what it  really becomes interesting is I can put  the 1937 Seaver map on top of it so this  is the map I'm told I wasn't alive in  1937 but I'm told that if you were  driving around and you didn't have a GPS  on your phone you would use one of these  maps and that's how you would figure out  where you were going and so what's  really fascinating is if we zoom in to a  specific part of the map you can see  that the photographers were actually  taking these classic routes like route  66 across the the southwest and you can  actually tracked photographers as they  were driving down the country and its  really interesting because if we click  here for instance this is jack delano  and he's in arizona and we see all of  these photographs were taken in march of  1943 that all of these photographs are  longing this route 66 were taken in  march of 1943 so what this was doing was  adding new context in New ordering a new  metadata that we're still trying to  figure out how can we incorporate it  back into the original photographs  because the the photographs as they sit  on the Library of Congress is just these  are all in march of 1943 but really  because we know where they were taken  and we have that data and we know that  he's went east to west because we have  some notes and letters and you can see  that i think i think he starts in march  and ends in April we know which  direction he went we actually know some  latent information about the photographs  we know that well unless he was driving  insanely and kind of popping up and down  and back and forth which he probably  wasn't he's probably going in some  systematic order down the highway and so  we know something about the ordering of  these photographs and so if you're  writing about how did jack delano view  the US and you wanted to write a  research paper about him it might be  really helpful to see well how did he  what sorts of things that he start  taking photos of what sort of things  that he stopped taking photos I would  like at the end of his trip and how did  that change and without this  visualization you wouldn't I don't even  know that I would have thought about  that piece of it and I certainly  wouldn't have been able to do it without  physically you know getting out a map  and marker and plotting them all out  which gets to be a lot when you have  165,000 photographs so those are just 